**Definition 6.1 (target, delivery and use indices).** An old-target body is indexed by its selected cut $k$ and actual source/code lineage. Its authentic delivery additionally has its cover, package, whole checked binding and receiver occurrence. A view of the currently executing carrier is indexed by the actual current cut and its retained frontier. In Theorem 3.1, $k=a$ is constant, $c_j$ varies with delivery, and $W_r$ varies at the current frontier. Corollary 4.1 requires an observation sufficient for current $Q$ to separate both displayed axes, although it need not store these particular coordinates.

Keeping the full acquired wrapper instead of only $D_a$ can preserve the recovery-round distinction. It cannot recover this request's later $w$: at fixed $j$ all that earlier acquired backing is already identical inside $P_A^+$. Conversely, selecting joint backing can distinguish $w$ mathematically without making it callable by $A$. These are different recovery conditions on the same actual carrier.

